.TH CADAPT 1 "2026-09-18" "cadapt 0.12.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cadapt \- compress and decompress files and folders with the adapt context-mixing codec
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cadapt encode
.I input
.RB [ \-o
.IR output ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.RB [ \-\-verify " | " \-\-no\-verify ]
.RB [ \-\-explain ]
.RB [ \-\-fast " | " \-\-mid " | " \-\-max ]
.RB [ \-0 " .. " \-11 ]
.RB [ \-\-allocation
.BR auto | window | context ]
.RB [ \-\-contexts
.BR auto | lean ]
.RB [ \-\-threads
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-\-segment\-bytes
.IR bytes ]
.RB [ \-\-adaptive
.BR rates | sparse | retained | auto ]
.RB [ \-\-transform
.BR bmp | text | none ]
.br
.B cadapt decode
.I input
.RB [ \-o
.IR output ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.RB [ \-\-max\-output
.IR bytes ]
.RB [ \-\-max\-memory
.IR bytes ]
.RB [ \-\-threads
.IR n ]
.br
.B cadapt info
.I file
.br
.B cadapt \-\-version
.br
.B cadapt \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
cadapt compresses arbitrary byte streams with an adaptive context model and
binary arithmetic coding. A container records the model and parameters needed
for decoding; no external dictionary or service is required.
.PP
Choose a profile for the predictor and a tier for its requested memory budget.
Compression ratio, speed and selected table sizes depend on the input.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI encode " input"
Compress a file, folder or standard input. The default output appends .adpt.
Named files carry mode and timestamps; folders also carry entries and supported
filesystem attributes. Use \-o to choose another output.
.TP
.BI decode " input"
Restore a container. The default output removes .adpt, or appends .out when
that suffix is absent. A folder requires a named output directory.
.TP
.BI info " file"
Print format, profile, declared sizes, model settings, transforms and decoder
memory requirements. This inspects a header; it does not prove payload integrity.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print command usage.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the release and format span, then exit. The span can contain unsupported
numbers; see COMPATIBILITY for the exact supported set.
.SH PROFILES AND TRANSFORMS
.TP
Default (normal)
General-purpose context modeling with first-block model selection.
.TP
.B \-\-fast
A predictor with fewer contexts and one arbiter.
.TP
.B \-\-mid
Seven fixed contexts and one arbiter. Cannot be combined with adaptive
predictors, BMP transformation or the lean context option.
.TP
.B \-\-max
Additional contexts, run models and arbiter policies. Model selection and sizing
fit the requested budget; a larger model is not a guarantee of fewer bytes.
.TP
.BR \-\-adaptive " " rates | sparse | retained | auto
Select an experimental causal predictor over normal or max. Rates uses
calibration experts, sparse adds lagged-byte count predictors, and retained
adds a bank selected from preceding block statistics.
.B auto
spools the input once, encodes the eligible base/adaptive/BMP candidates and
keeps the smallest complete output. Ties favor the earlier candidate. Up to
eight sequential encodes use temporary disk for input and two containers.
.TP
.BR \-\-transform " " bmp | text | none
BMP applies reversible color and row residuals to eligible uncompressed 24-bit
BMP data. Headers, padding and unmatched bytes remain unchanged.
Text builds a dictionary causally from repeated words in the input. Eligible
text of known length at least 8 MiB uses it automatically; an unknown-length
pipe needs an explicit request. None disables automatic transforms.
Text cannot be combined with adaptive predictors or BMP transformation.
.SH MEMORY AND MODEL OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-0 " .. " \-11
Select the requested encoding budget. The default is \-9; the last tier wins.
.TS
l l l l l l l.
tier	-0	-1	-2	-3	-4	-5
MiB	3	9	17	32	48	94
.TE
.TS
l l l l l l l.
tier	-6	-7	-8	-9	-10	-11
MiB	94	186	370	674	1024	2048
.TE
.IP
Requests below the profile's smallest sizing budget are raised to it.
Normal needs 28,184,711 bytes; max needs 57,816,199 bytes for that minimum
request. Selected model memory may be lower. A tier covers codec model,
window and workspace, not every process allocation. Folder enumeration,
metadata, verification and segmented operation have additional costs.
.IP
Decoder memory follows the model recorded in the container; it is not the
requested encoder tier. Inspect the requirement with cadapt info.
.TP
.BR \-\-allocation " " auto | window | context
Choose how sizing divides memory between the context table and recall window.
Auto grows the table first when window-first sizing leaves it below the
input's target; otherwise it uses window-first sizing. Window or context
forces that preference. A segment is the sizing unit for segmented encoding.
.TP
.BR \-\-contexts " " auto | lean
Auto compares eligible context sets on the first block. Lean forces the reduced
set, omitting word/field contexts and additional max-profile contexts.
Unavailable with fast or mid.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-memory " bytes"
Decode only: refuse a model whose working-memory requirement exceeds the cap.
The decoder cannot shrink a recorded model. No cap applies unless supplied.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-output " bytes"
Decode only: cap decoded bytes and, for archives, the combined logical size of
restored files, including sparse holes and hard-link aliases. Declared sizes
are checked before model/result allocation; stream and archive checks occur
as lengths become available. No cap applies unless supplied.
.SH OUTPUT, VERIFICATION AND PARALLELISM
.TP
.BI \-o " output"
Select the output path. Input and output can each be \- for a byte stream.
A folder cannot be restored to standard output.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Permit replacement of an existing output. Named outputs remain staged until
publication. Forced folder replacement depends on the platform/filesystem:
Linux/macOS support atomic rename or exchange for supported destination types;
Windows refuses an existing folder destination. An unsupported replacement
fails without deleting the destination.
.TP
.BR \-\-verify ", " \-\-no\-verify
Encoder round-trip proof is off by default. Verification uses a second model
when the budget permits, or a second pass with one model for suitable named-file
input/output. A pipe that cannot fit the required proof resources is refused.
Candidate search verifies its selected output when this option is enabled.
.TP
.B \-\-explain
Report allocation, contexts, arbiter keys, transform choices and trial sizes
on standard error. This does not change the container. Diagnostic field layout
may change between releases.
.TP
.BI \-\-threads " n"
Request workers, default 1. Encoding with n of at least two creates segments,
each starting a fresh model. Sizes are fitted to the requested count; actual
workers are limited by memory and available CPUs. Changing the requested count
can change compressed bytes. Scheduling the same segments differently cannot.
A segmented file can be decoded in parallel; pipe decoding uses a stream.
.TP
.BI \-\-segment\-bytes " bytes"
Select segment length for threaded encoding. The default is 16777216 bytes;
lengths must be whole 262144-byte blocks. Restarting a model can reduce ratio.
.SH FILESYSTEM METADATA AND SAFETY
Named files and folders preserve modes and access/modification times.
POSIX timestamps retain nanoseconds; applying and reading back required
metadata must succeed. Folder entries include supported links, sparse extents,
attributes and ACLs. Ownership, creation time and status-change time are not
preserved.
.PP
Linux records user attributes and POSIX access/default ACLs. macOS records
extended attributes but not macOS ACLs. Windows records runtime modes and
whole-second times; it rejects symlinks and attribute-bearing archives, and
restores hard-link entries as copies. Windows behavior is not validated.
Sparse storage depends on the destination filesystem; logical bytes are preserved.
.PP
POSIX named outputs use private staging wrappers and retained handles.
Folder metadata replay uses temporary disk with bounded working memory after
checksum validation. Existing destinations are preserved until replacement
succeeds. Catchable failures attempt cleanup; uncatchable termination or
cleanup failure can leave temporary entries. Errors after publication report
that state and can retain a replaced entry for recovery.
.PP
Standard output and library callbacks can receive bytes before a final failure.
Accept their output only on success. Checksums detect corruption; they do not
authenticate or encrypt data. Resource caps are opt-in.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
Supported container versions: 19-54, 56-58, 61-72, 75-85.
The CLI writes archive version 4 and reads archive versions 1-4.
Containers from cadapt 0.11.0 (versions 13-15) require that release to decode;
convert them or keep the old binary before upgrading. Unknown numbers are rejected.
.PP
Small-table model2 containers in versions 58/61 can identify two predictors.
Decoding tries them sequentially with one live model and temporary disk for
tentative bytes; output is emitted only after checksum verification.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ADAPT_ISA
Set to baseline to select the portable predictor path. It produces the same
bytes as the accelerated path for identical input, metadata and settings.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Success.
.TP
1
Codec, resource or I/O failure. A stream output may contain a prefix.
.TP
2
Invalid command usage.
.TP
128 + signal number
Handled POSIX interruption: SIGINT, SIGTERM, SIGHUP, SIGPIPE or SIGXFSZ.
Blocked I/O wakes for cleanup; active codec work reaches its next I/O boundary.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cadapt encode report.pdf
cadapt decode report.pdf.adpt -o report-copy.pdf
cadapt encode photos/ -o photos.adpt --verify
cadapt decode photos.adpt -o photos-copy
cadapt encode records.csv --max -6
cadapt info records.csv.adpt
cadapt decode records.csv.adpt -o records-copy.csv --max-memory 134217728
printf 'example data\en' | cadapt encode - --fast -3 > example.adpt
cadapt decode - < example.adpt
.fi
